Cron is a time-based job scheduling utility in Linux and Unix-like operating systems. It runs as a background service (known as a daemon named crond or cron) that automatically executes scheduled tasks, scripts, or commands at predefined times without manual user intervention.

System administrators and developers primarily use cron to automate routine operations such as system backups, log rotation, software updates, and periodic data synchronization.


1. How Cron Works Under the Hood

• The Background Daemon: When a Linux system boots up, the initialization system (like systemd) starts the cron or crond daemon process.

systemctl status crond 

• The Minute-by-Minute Check: The daemon wakes up every 60 seconds to examine the configuration files where scheduled tasks are defined.
• Time Matching: It compares the current system time against the rules set by the user. If a rule matches the system time exactly, it spawns a background sub-shell to execute the specified command.

• Output Handling: 
By default, if a command generates an output or an error, cron attempts to email it to the local system owner unless explicitly instructed to redirect it somewhere else. 
If no mail system is configured, these details are typically discarded or routed to system logs var/log/cron.
usually /var/log/cron.

cron will use the permissions of the user who is scheduling the task to execute task background, so the user who is scheduling must have execute permissions to that command or file under execution schedule

